---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Index from '../components/Index.astro';
import Redacted from '../components/Redacted.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Who made this"
  description="Why the project exists, who is behind it, how to contribute, and what it is paid for with."
  status="draft"
  here="/about"
>
  <section class="band band--first">
    <h1 class="statement">We built this because the meter was missing.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Every other utility in your life has a meter on it. Electricity, water, petrol, mobile data.
      The one thing people now use dozens of times a day has no dial at all, and the published
      figures for it disagree by more than an order of magnitude.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="band__head">
      <Index n={1} of={4} />
      <h2 class="band__title">Why</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Two bad answers were already on offer. One says the cost is so small it rounds to nothing,
        which is true for one prompt and stops being true somewhere between the tenth and the
        thousandth. The other says a single question boils a kettle, which is wrong by a factor
        nobody bothers to check. Both are a way of not looking.
      </p>
      <p>
        The honest answer is that it depends, and that the range is wide, and that the range itself
        is the interesting part. A frontier model asked to do arithmetic costs a few hundred times
        what a small one costs to do the same arithmetic. That gap is worth seeing, and it is the
        only part of this anyone can act on.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the tool is built to be uncomfortable to misuse. It will not give you one number. It puts
        a source one click from every figure, marks the ones that are scaled from somebody else's
        measurement, and says "unknown" when it does not know, rather than zero.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="band__head">
      <Index n={2} of={4} />
      <h2 class="band__title">Who</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Anirudh Sharma. Affiliation: <Redacted>still to be written</Redacted>. On GitHub as{' '}
        <a href="https://github.com/gordianknot-legacy">gordianknot-legacy</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nobody pays for this. There is no company behind it, no funding, no sponsor, and nothing is
        sold. If that changes it will be written here first.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="band__head">
      <Index n={3} of={4} />
      <h2 class="band__title">Helping</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The most useful contribution by a distance is a benchmark row: a published measurement we
        have missed, with its boundary and its method written down. The second most useful is
        telling us a row is wrong, with a reason.
      </p>
      <p>
        Code, adapters for surfaces we do not cover yet, and translations are all welcome.
        <code>docs/dataset-contributing.md</code> in the repository sets out what a row needs before
        it can go in.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="band__head">
      <Index n={4} of={4} />
      <h2 class="band__title">Licence and credit</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        MIT, for the code and for the dataset structure. The measurements themselves belong to the
        people who made them and each row names its source.
      </p>
      <p>
        Several projects got here first and shaped what this one does: AI Impact Tracker, EcoLogits,
        AI Wattch, GreenChat, carbon-llm, GPTFootprint, claude-carbon and ccusage. Where one of them
        is under the GPL we read it and credited it, and wrote our own. The README lists them all.
      </p>
      <p>
        Grid intensities come from Ember and the EPA, which permit reuse with credit. Electricity
        Maps data is not used anywhere, because its licence would spread to the rest of the dataset.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
